 ### Memory Requirements
 Some examples may exceed available memory on older Arduino boards. The library is optimized for modern Arduino boards with adequate RAM.
 
+## Choosing float or Float16
+
+Keep `float` (the default) when RAM is sufficient, you need more numerical precision or a wider range, or CPU time and flash matter more than sample storage. Existing sketches need no changes to keep using float.
+
+Use `grafici::Float16` when sample arrays take too much RAM and lower precision is acceptable. Each stored value takes **2 bytes instead of 4**, with roughly three significant decimal digits and a maximum finite magnitude of 65,504. It supports negative values and values above 1; arithmetic is not clamped to `[0,1]`. Calculations use float and round when stored as Float16. Conversion can add CPU work and flash usage, so this is a RAM-saving choice, not a promise of faster drawing.
+
+### Start by changing the sample storage
+
+Change your array's element type and the first template argument of `DataArray` or `DataArrayXY`. Keep `Grafici`, windows and options unchanged. Assuming `display` is your configured Adafruit GFX display:
+
+```cpp
+#include "Grafici.h"
+
+using Sample = grafici::Float16; // Change to float to restore FP32 storage.
+Sample values[] = {-2.0f, 0.5f, 1.5f, 3.0f};
+DataArrayXY<Sample> data(values, 4, {-4, 4});
+Grafici plot(display);
+
+void draw_graph()
+{
+    auto x = data.x();
+    auto y = data.y();
+    plot.line(x, y, white);
+}
+```
+
+This halves the sample buffer while keeping range metadata, normalization and rendering in float. In a matched Uno R3 build with 128 samples, this saved 256 bytes of static RAM and added 596 bytes of flash. Keep integer sample buffers as integers if they already use less space.
+
+### Select Float16 throughout the pipeline
+
+To also narrow value/range objects, window parameters and retained spline coefficients, select the same scalar for sources and plotters:
+
+```cpp
+using Real = grafici::Float16; // Change this alias to float to switch back.
+Real values[] = {-2.0f, 0.5f, 1.5f, 3.0f};
+DataArrayXY<Real, Real> data(values, 4, {-4, 4});
+GraficiT<Real> plot(display);
+```
+
+Drawing calls stay the same. Explicit windows and options become `WindowT<Real>` and `PlotOptionsT<Real>`; use `full_screen_for<Real>()` where you previously passed `full_screen`. Existing template classes accept the scalar as their final argument, for example `DataSpline<32, true, Real>`. Custom sources use `DataSourceNormT<Real>`.
+
+Narrowing every object does not always give the smallest program. Keep float for closely spaced values on a large offset, sensitive interpolation, or values outside FP16's finite range. Spline solver scratch arrays remain float. See the [precision guide](PRECISION.md) for limits, measured sizes and the full type mapping, or the [complete FP16 sketch](examples/fp16_line/fp16_line.ino).
+
 ## Testing
 
 ### Unit Tests
